helps us do is identify things that are important in one document compared to another document.

00:46:33.093 --> 00:46:39.572
It's not the only way to do this. So we just talked about this term frequency. But another

00:46:39.572 --> 00:46:46.213
way that I find very impactful for this kind of work is what's called a weighted log odds.

00:46:46.773 --> 00:46:51.973
Now, we're not even really to models yet. But we're starting to have a little bit more

00:46:51.973 --> 00:46:59.413
sophistication in what we're doing. So I imagine that you all are familiar that a log odds ratio

00:46:59.413 --> 00:47:08.692
tells us a probability. And we can weight the probabilities because we know the distribution

00:47:08.692 --> 00:47:16.533
of words. So remember that some words are super common. Other words are more rare. And we want to

00:47:16.533 --> 00:47:25.973
weight the ‑‑ we want to weight the log odds ratio by how sure we are that what we're seeing

00:47:25.973 --> 00:47:33.173
is a real difference. So let's say ‑‑ let's say we have some words that are very common, like

00:47:33.173 --> 00:47:41.733
and and K. You know? If we see a difference in those words, a big difference, we know that is

00:47:41.733 --> 00:47:47.252
real. But let's say there's a word that appears one time in one book and doesn't appear in the

00:47:47.252 --> 00:47:53.572
other book. We cannot be so certain that that's a significant difference. We have to weight. Because

00:47:53.572 --> 00:48:01.892
natural language exhibits a power law distribution. It's, you know, we ‑‑ if you plot it on a log

00:48:01.892 --> 00:48:09.413
log scale, we see that some words we use many times and other words we use very little. It's a

00:48:09.413 --> 00:48:14.773
characteristic of text data that we have this power law distribution. So when we use weighted

00:48:14.773 --> 00:48:23.012
log odds, it helps us to get a good answer for ‑‑ a good answer for which words have a

00:48:23.012 --> 00:48:28.533
higher probability from coming from one document for the others. For those of you who are statisticians

00:48:28.533 --> 00:48:34.213
in the bunch, the typical way of doing weighted log odds is with empirical Bayes. So empirical

00:48:34.213 --> 00:48:40.452
Bayes, maybe we're starting to say is a model, you know, but it just uses the data itself to be

00:48:40.452 --> 00:48:45.173
able to ‑‑ to be able to do the weighting, to be able to say what is the distribution that we

00:48:45.173 --> 00:48:51.812
would have. So we're maybe starting to get into using some modeling, but not so much. So it looks

00:48:51.812 --> 00:49:00.132
just the same as a bind TFIDF, whereas here we're doing bind log odds. It is in another package in

00:49:00.132 --> 00:49:05.732
tidy log, because this actually works great, not just for text data, but for many kinds of data

00:49:05.732 --> 00:49:11.573
that we can do this kind of weighted log odds. Anytime we have like a power law distribution,

00:49:11.573 --> 00:49:17.252
it's a really great application. Definitely true in text that we have that. So this is a different

00:49:17.252 --> 00:49:24.212
statistic. So the statistic before was TFIDF. It's a heuristic statistic, but it's one that we can

00:49:24.212 --> 00:49:30.693
measure for a word in a document in a collection of documents. This is a different thing we can

00:49:30.693 --> 00:49:36.212
measure, the log odds. So it gives us ‑‑ you'll notice here we have some similar results, you know,

00:49:36.212 --> 00:49:44.693
eugenia, you said, panza, you know, Dulcinea, we have some similar words, but it's a different

00:49:44.693 --> 00:49:53.573
approach, but the main difference is that weighted log odds can distinguish between words that are

00:49:53.573 --> 00:50:00.372
used in all documents, in all text. So remember that when we had K in all three of the books,

00:50:00.372 --> 00:50:06.612
it went down to zero. But a weighted log odds allows us to distinguish between words that are

00:50:06.612 --> 00:50:11.892
in all the text. And they do sometimes ‑‑ you can say, oh, this word is much more important in

00:50:11.892 --> 00:50:18.132
this document than this document, even if it is in all the documents. Okay.

