01:47:53.908 --> 01:48:00.908
distance measurements, say, cosine similarity or something more significant of a different way of measuring the distances.

01:48:00.908 --> 01:48:12.908
You can see that in the high dimensional space, words like mother, aunt, all kinds of feminine words are closer, say, to family words,

01:48:12.908 --> 01:48:22.908
and words that are specific to men, say, father, uncle. These are some of the classic ones. King and queen is another classic one.

01:48:23.908 --> 01:48:38.908
Real quick. King minus man equals queen. But the problem is, that's good. Doctor minus man equals nurse is terrible, but you find this result.

01:48:38.908 --> 01:48:46.908
You can do these kinds of math with the words because they are represented in this high dimensional space.

01:48:47.908 --> 01:48:58.908
If you think about it, how can you quantify this kind of bias? In terms of how else can we quantify the bias is that we see over and over and over again,

01:48:58.908 --> 01:49:11.908
when we use them in a system, we end up with results that I would say are bias in the colloquial sense, that you're like, that system now is not being fair.

01:49:12.908 --> 01:49:22.908
We would say behaving unfairly. Embeddings like this are behind much automatic translation.

01:49:22.908 --> 01:49:32.908
If you go to Google Translate, embeddings like this are what are used to map from one language to another, to be able to map.

01:49:33.908 --> 01:49:43.908
Sure, you've seen examples like this of translating from a language that does not have pronouns with gender to one that has pronouns with gender and back again,

01:49:43.908 --> 01:49:53.908
and you end up in a loop. The doctor-nurse one has happened so many times. You can construct these things like,

01:49:54.908 --> 01:50:05.908
she is a doctor, and then you translate it to something with no gender, like that person is a doctor, and then you translate it back and it's like he is a doctor.

01:50:05.908 --> 01:50:17.908
That's more qualitative. We see it in both ways. You can measure it using the math of the embeddings, and then we see it in the way the systems behave once you use them in the systems.

01:50:18.908 --> 01:50:33.908
The second question was the following one. You have a high dimensional space, but maybe using multidimensional scaling you can reduce, or whatever,

01:50:34.908 --> 01:50:48.908
dimensional technique for reducing the dimensionality of the product. You can reduce the product to some dimensions related with cheese, with whatever.

01:50:49.908 --> 01:51:14.908
If you can represent the text in that less dimensional space, my question is, why not use distances to translate our space, cheese space,

01:51:15.908 --> 01:51:34.908
in a physical space, where we could use all the instruments of just statistics, and some new possibilities are open.

01:51:34.908 --> 01:51:52.908
The algorithms for finding word embeddings are more efficient for text than, say, the generic dimensionality reduction.

01:51:52.908 --> 01:52:02.908
I said it's like PCA in analogy, but PCA on words does not work as well at all as these text-specific algorithms.

01:52:02.908 --> 01:52:18.908
A simple one that is a little bit of a better example is you look for pairwise information for sliding windows along a text, and you can use that, and then you can do dimensionality reduction here.

01:52:19.908 --> 01:52:33.908
I was like glove, word2vec, glove, fast text, and now these very more sophisticated approaches.

01:52:33.908 --> 01:52:51.908
The reason why people develop these rather than use the very straightforward, or not very, but dimensionality reduction that comes from more classic areas of statistics is because of how sparse text data is,

01:52:51.908 --> 01:53:03.908
and also that language has rules underneath it, and so you use the rules of language to do the dimension, you learn them specifically.

01:53:03.908 --> 01:53:15.908
The algorithms are a little different because they are used to take advantage of the rules that is inherent in language to get the representation.

01:53:15.908 --> 01:53:22.908
Once you have the representation, you can do anything that you might do with another matrix.

01:53:22.908 --> 01:53:29.908
You can use them as features for training a supervised model.

01:53:29.908 --> 01:53:36.908
Say you have a bunch of documents, say you have a bunch of cheeses, and then thumbs up, thumbs down on the cheese.

